{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>A man — his mother and his father you shall fear, and My Sabbaths you shall keep.</b> Rashi explained: if your father says to you, 'Desecrate the Sabbath,' do not listen to him. And in parashat Va-yakhel, Rashi explained from the fact that the keeping of Sabbath is set before the building of the Tabernacle — to tell you that the building of the Tabernacle does not push aside the Sabbath; infer from this that the earlier one pushes aside the later one. Here it is different, for it begins in singular language: 'a man — his mother and his father,' and concludes in plural language — 'you shall fear,' 'you shall keep.' Now, it is fine that 'you shall fear' refers to man and woman, as Rashi explained; but regarding the Sabbath, why did He say 'you shall keep' in plural language — what difference is it to me whether man or woman? Rather, surely 'you shall keep' refers to the son and the father, to tell you that you and your father are both obligated in My honor." "And as for why He specified the Sabbath more than any other commandment in the Torah — it is because the reason for the commandment of honoring father and mother is that it is fitting to give honor to the one who brought you into the light of existence: surely your father acquired you and he made you, so how could you not give honor to the one who brought you into being, for without him you would have no existence at all? And the Sabbath testifies to the honor of HaShem, blessed be He, for it indicates the creation anew of the world and that the Holy One, blessed be He, brought you and your father into the light of existence. And if your father says to you, 'Desecrate the Sabbath,' in order to plant in your heart that the world is primordial and not created anew, so that you will believe that you have no other father besides him, and he says all this to entice you to nullify the will of your Father in Heaven for the sake of his will — do not consent and do not listen to him. For in truth He, blessed be He, is a father to all of us, and you are obligated to nullify the will of your father for the sake of His will, blessed be He, for you and your father are both obligated in His honor. But if your father says to you to transgress a different transgression, it is obvious that you should not listen to him, as long as the root of your faith remains intact and you keep the Sabbath according to its law and believe in the creation anew of the world — for there is one Father to all of us — and regarding this He does not need to command you; only if he comes to dispute the very root of faith from the outset, regarding this you need extra admonition. And therefore we find in several places the juxtaposition of the Sabbath to the commandment of honoring father and mother, for the Sabbath too testifies to the honor of the first Father of all that was formed. And the juxtaposition further includes the three partners that are in man: father and mother and the Holy One, blessed be He." "And as for why, regarding fear, He puts the mother before the father, while regarding honor, He puts the father before the mother — it is because the husband too is obligated to honor his wife, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Yevamot 62b): 'A person should always honor his wife more than his own self.' It might enter your mind that there is not such strictness if you slight her honor, since the father too is obligated to honor your mother; therefore He put the father first, to tell you that his honor should be great upon you like the honor of the mother, even though the two of you are both obligated in the honor of the mother. And regarding fear, it is necessary that the fear of her husband be upon her; it might enter your mind that her fear does not carry such strictness, and therefore He put the mother first, to tell you that in any event her fear is upon you."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:27.529785801Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}